# Line endings and file types.
#
# .editorconfig already asks editors for LF; this asks git for the same, so a
# checkout on Windows cannot quietly turn a plugin file into CRLF and hand a
# Zen Cart store a file that differs byte-for-byte from the one that was tested.
* text=auto eol=lf

# THE PDF MUST BE BINARY.
#
# Not a precaution -- git gets this one wrong here. Its heuristic calls a file
# text when the first 8000 bytes hold no NUL byte, and the printable sign-up
# sheet is hand-built with an UNCOMPRESSED content stream, so it is almost
# entirely ASCII and contains no NUL at all. Left to that heuristic git offers
# to "fix" its line endings, which rewrites bytes inside the file and shifts
# every offset in its cross-reference table. The result is a PDF that viewers
# refuse to open, produced by nothing more than cloning the repository on a
# different platform.
*.pdf binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.zip binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.eot binary
